Reports <code>System.out.println()</code> and <code>IO.println()</code> calls which can be replaced with a Logpoint.
Logpoints are a debugger feature to print expressions to standard output <b>without</b> requiring changes to the source code.
The advantages of Logpoints:
<ul>
<li>Can be added, modified, and removed during execution, no need to HotSwap</li>
<li>Can be used in library code, which usually can't be changed</li>
<li>When a Logpoint contains invalid (uncompilable) code, the program remains runnable</li>
<li>No risk of leaking printing calls into production, as logpoints are not part of the source code</li>
</ul>

The inspection only reports when there's another executable line available, that is not a loop statement, to put the Logpoint.
<br>
<b>NOTE:</b> Logpoints only work when Debugging. Under Run, Run with Coverage and Profile modes, logpoint expressions won't appear in the output.
